# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Frontend configuration — copy to `frontend/.env.local` and edit.
#
# This is the WHOLE list. The frontend reads exactly these variables and nothing else; anything
# else you find in a search of this directory belongs to Next.js itself or to a dependency.
#
# Nothing here is a secret, and nothing here may become one. Every variable below is compiled
# into the JavaScript the browser downloads (that is what the NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix means), so a
# value placed here is published. API keys, database URLs and session secrets belong in
# backend/.env, which the browser never sees.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Where the backend is. This is the ONE variable a normal local setup needs.
#
# It must be an origin the BROWSER can reach, not one only the Next.js server can: these requests
# are made by the page, cross-origin, with credentials. The backend's CORS_ORIGIN must name this
# app's origin in return, or the browser will refuse every call — including the login that would
# otherwise tell you something is wrong.
#
# Default when unset: http://localhost:8787 (backend/.env.example's PORT).
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:8787

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# End-to-end tests only (frontend/playwright.config.ts). Leave both unset for ordinary work:
# `npm run test:e2e` starts its own backend on 8790 and its own frontend on 3100.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Point the Playwright run at an API that is already running instead of starting one.
# E2E_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8790

# Point the Playwright run at a frontend that is already running instead of building one.
# E2E_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3100

# An explicit Chromium binary, for a machine where `npx playwright install` cannot be run.
# PLAYWRIGHT_CHROMIUM_PATH=
